  "title": "A failing runs-history request must not block the worker cards",
  "file": "ui/pages/Workers.tsx",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "A user opens the Workers page while the history endpoint is unavailable, including when an older daemon serves the requests. Successful worker information is discarded on every refresh, leaving the cards unavailable and the page showing a loading message.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun test /var/folders/dx/mlv6nn_15619ymmlszfmkywm0000gn/T/adjudicate-workers-xra9ewyc/workers-history-404.test.ts",
    "output": "FIRST LOAD: {\"text\":\"Workersnot foundloading\u2026Runs that used workers\",\"workerVisible\":false,\"loading\":true}\nAFTER REFRESH: {\"text\":\"Workersnot foundloading\u2026Runs that used workers\",\"workerVisible\":false,\"loading\":true}\n1 pass, 0 fail, 5 expect() calls. The throwaway test asserts the observed defective behavior."
   },
   "evidence": "The throwaway test mounts HEAD's actual Workers component using the existing renderer and API request implementation, supplies successful workers responses and history HTTP 404 responses, then advances the refresh timer. Neither worker response renders. HEAD awaits 'Promise.all([getWorkers(), getWorkerRuns()])' before calling 'setWorkers(workers)'; a history rejection therefore skips both setters and reaches the shared error handler. Base has '/api/workers' but no history endpoint and no Workers component, establishing both the older-daemon scenario and that the coupling is newly introduced."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "If the run-history request fails while the workers request succeeds, the page throws away the good worker data. This can happen when a browser tab is still open after a rollback to a daemon without that endpoint, or on a server error. The worker cards then stay on \"loading\u2026\" or freeze at old data, with an error shown, until the history request works again or the tab is reloaded.",
   "reproduction": "unreproduced: no throwaway UI test was run. The mechanism is plain from the code at head. In normal operation the same daemon serves the UI and both endpoints, so the realistic trigger is a stale tab after a rollback, or a transient server error.",
   "evidence": "ui/pages/Workers.tsx:13-17 at head: `const [workers, runs] = await Promise.all([getWorkers(), getWorkerRuns()]); if (request !== generation) return; setWorkers(workers); setRuns(runs);`. The catch only calls setError. If getWorkerRuns rejects, setWorkers is never called even though getWorkers succeeded, and `<Show when={workers()} fallback={<div>loading\u2026</div>}>` keeps the fallback on first load. The UI is bundled into and served by the daemon (buildUi), which limits when the two endpoints can disagree, so the severity is minor."
  }
